#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:68
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kworldclock;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:70
#, no-c-format
msgid "The main display"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:71
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you start &kworldclock;, you will see a small window appear, as shown "
"below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here's a screenshot of &kworldclock;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:84
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot"
msgstr "Ecran"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:90
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The light section (which in the screenshot above covers Antartica, "
"Australia, eastern and southern Africa and most of Asia; plus the Indian "
"Ocean and the western part of the Pacific Ocean) shows the area that is "
"currently experiencing daylight, while the darker section (which in the "
"screenshot above covers western Europe, North and South America, and the "
"Arctic zone) shows the area that is currently experiencing night. The border "
"between the dark and light section is the area that is experiencing morning "
"or evening twilight."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Each small black square indicates a city or population centre. If you locate "
"the mouse over (or near) one of the black squares, you will see the date and "
"time that is current for that location. In the screenshot above, the tiny "
"island of Pitcairn is shown."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You may find it more convenient to expand the window, so that there is more "
"space between the various squares. Remember that you can expand the window "
"by either clicking on the maximise button (second button from the right in "
"the top right hand corner) or by using the mouse to drag the corner of the "
"main window. An expanded window, with Melbourne (Australia) now selected, is "
"shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; with expanded window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing expanded window, with Melbourne selected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:134
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuring &kworldclock; with timezone clocks"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:136
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you need to know the time in a number of areas around the world, it may "
"be useful to have &kworldclock; show the applicable times. An example of how "
"this can look is shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:144
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; with clocks"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing expanded window, with three clocks."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:156
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note the clocks for Perth, Sydney and Berlin at the bottom of the main "
"&kworldclock; window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"These clocks are added by using the right mouse button, and selecting the "
"<guilabel>Clock</guilabel> entry, as shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:168
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; &RMB; clock"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:174
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing &RMB; menu, with <guilabel>Clock</guilabel> selected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:181
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This will allow access to the <guilabel>Add...</guilabel> selection, which "
"will bring up a dialog box as shown below. The contents of the dialog box "
"are determined from the closest city to the mouse cursor when you make the "
"selection."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:190
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; <guilabel>Edit Clock Settings</guilabel> dialog"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:197
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing<guilabel>Edit Clock Settings</guilabel> dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:205
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can now make an appropriate selection for the clock label and the "
"timezone. Note that you can change the label to whatever is appropriate, and "
"the timezone as required. An example, for the small Australian town of "
"Quairading, is shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:214
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Screenshot of &kworldclock; <guilabel>Edit Clock Settings</guilabel> dialog "
"changed to Quairading"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:221
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Screenshot showing <guilabel>Edit Clock Settings</guilabel> dialog for "
"Quairading."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:228
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you select OK, the clock entry is added to those at the bottom of the "
"main window. A screenshot, with the extra entry for Quairading, is shown "
"below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:236
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; with Quairading clock"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:242
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing Quairading clock."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:248
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You might notice that Perth and Quairading are now showing the same "
"information (since they are in the same timezone). In this case, the Perth "
"entry is no longer required, so it can be removed. This can be done by again "
"using the right mouse button on the clock display, and selecting the "
"<guilabel>Remove</guilabel> item."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can also edit entries by using the <guilabel>Edit...</guilabel> entry on "
"right mouse button menu, which may be useful for making minor changes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:264
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding Flags to the &kworldclock; display"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:266
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As shown in the screenshot below, you can add flags to places on the map (in "
"this case, Fremantle near Perth, Western Australia; near Moscow in Russia; "
"and the Azores in the mid-Atlantic). These flags don't affect any other "
"functionality - they just allows you to quickly locate places of interest."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:276
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; with flags"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:282
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing expanded window, with three flags."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:288
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You add flags by locating the mouse at the point where you want to appear, "
"and using the right mouse button to select the the <guilabel>Flags...</"
"guilabel> entry. You can select one of the three pre-defined flag colours "
"(that is, <guilabel>Add Red</guilabel>, <guilabel>Add Green</guilabel> or "
"<guilabel>Add Blue</guilabel>), or you can select a custom colour."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:297
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You use the same menu action to remove just one flag (whichever one is "
"closest to the mouse when you make the <guilabel>Remove Flag</guilabel> "
"selection; or to remove all the flags, using the <guilabel>Remove All Flags</"
"guilabel>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:306
#, no-c-format
msgid "Changing the &kworldclock; display"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:308
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In addition to the additional clocks and flags, there are other minor "
"variations you can make to the main &kworldclock; display, such as not "
"showing the flags, not showing the small black city squares, and not showing "
"the day/night areas. Each of these is selected using the right mouse button "
"menu."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:316
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To enable and disable the display of the day / night sections, you use the "
"right mouse button, and select the <guilabel>Show Daylight</guilabel> item "
"from the menu. Each time you select it, the selection will be toggled. When "
"showing day and night is enabled (normal mode on first startup), there will "
"be a small check mark (tick symbol) next to the <guilabel>Show Daylight</"
"guilabel>. An example of the menu item (with day / night not enabled, hence "
"no check mark) is shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:329
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; &RMB; daylight"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:335
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Screenshot showing &RMB; menu, with <guilabel>Show Daylight</guilabel> "
"selected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:343
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To enable and disable the display of the small black city squares, you use "
"the right mouse button, and select the <guilabel>Show Cities</guilabel> item "
"from the menu. Each time you select it, the selection will be toggled. When "
"showing cities is enabled (normal mode on first startup), there will be a "
"small check mark (tick symbol) next to the <guilabel>Show Cities</guilabel>. "
"An example of the menu item (with city display enabled, hence a check mark) "
"is shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:356
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; &RMB; show cities"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:362
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Screenshot showing &RMB; menu, with <guilabel>Show Cities</guilabel> "
"selected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:369
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To enable and disable the display of the flags, you use the right mouse "
"button, and select the <guilabel>Show Flags</guilabel> item from the menu. "
"Each time you select it, the selection will be toggled. When showing flags "
"is enabled (normal mode on first startup), there will be a small check mark "
"(tick symbol) next to the <guilabel>Show Flags</guilabel>. An example of the "
"menu item (with flags enabled, hence a check mark) is shown below."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:382
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot of &kworldclock; &RMB; show flags"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:388
#, no-c-format
msgid "Screenshot showing &RMB; menu, with <guilabel>Show Flags</guilabel> selected."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:395
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After reconfiguring &kworldclock;, you may wish to save your settings. This "
"is done using the right mouse button, and selecting the <guilabel>Save "
"Settings</guilabel> option."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:403
#, no-c-format
msgid "Selecting alternative maps"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:404
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In addition to the normal surface depth map, you can add additional maps, "
"which can be selected using the <guilabel>Map Theme</guilabel> item from the "
"right mouse button menu. &kworldclock; comes with a single map, which "
"provides surface depth (relief). Additional maps can be obtained from <ulink "
"url=\"http://www.radcyberzine.com/xglobe\">http://www.radcyberzine.com/"
"xglobe</ulink>. Maps need to be appropriately installed - typically into "
"<filename>share/apps/kworldclock/maps</filename> under the main &kde; "
"directory. Consult your system administrator for assistance in doing this, "
"if required."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:416
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you want to use your own map, you should make sure that Greenwich is "
"exactly in the middle of the map, and provide an appropriate desktop file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:427
#, no-c-format
msgid "Developer's Guide to &kworldclock;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:429
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The most common requirement for development of &kworldclock; is to add "
"additional cities to the map. The cities are determined by a file named "
"<filename>zone.tab</filename>, which is located under <filename>share/apps/"
"kworldclock</filename> under the main &kde; directory."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:437
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To add an additional city, you need to know the latitude and longitude, and "
"also the applicable time zone information. There is additional information "
"on the exact format required in the <filename>zone.tab</filename> file."
msgstr ""

